    "texts":[
        {
            "texttype":"Online",
            "textpurpose":"Provenance",
            "textentryhtml":"John Skippe (1742-1811). [Christie’s, London, November 20, 1958, lot 212]; [Mathias Komor, New York, February 29, 1964, $280]; purchased by Gillett Griffin (1928-2016); bequeathed to the Princeton University Art Museum, 2016.\n",
            "remarks":"As per Giada Damen, IMLS Drawings 2018-2020. Reformatted by Emma Holter, summer 2025."
        },
        {
            "texttype":"Online",
            "textpurpose":"Gallery Label",
            "textentryhtml":"When Tibaldi arrived in Rome in 1547, a part of his education as a young artist was to copy major monuments, such as Michelangelo’s frescoes in the Sistine Chapel. This drawing is an adaptation of an idealized Ignudo (nude) found in the border of the ceiling panel that depicts Noah’s Sacrifice after the Flood. In the sixteenth century Michelangelo’s Ignudi were often copied by Mannerist artists, who were inspired by the dramatic gestures and sculptural magnificence of the figures.",
            "remarks":"Tibaldi May 2012.pdf"
        }
